sssd-smartcard (0.3.6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixes a misdiagnosis introduced by 0.3.5's own fix. That release taught the
    card-reading error to say which link in the chain broke, and then guessed
    "contention" whenever a card was present in the reader -- including when
    the operator had asked for a slot that does not exist. Pointing
    'cert inspect --from-card --slot 99' at a healthy card produced advice
    about login and screen-lock helpers, which is the same misdirection the
    message was written to remove.
  * When --slot or --module narrowed the search by hand, the message now names
    what was asked for and says to check that it exists. Contention is offered
    only when nothing was narrowed, which is the case it was written for and
    which still works. Found by exercising the error branch on the host rather
    than trusting that a message about misdiagnosis could not itself
    misdiagnose.

 -- Szymon Frankiewicz <szymonfrankiewicz@gmail.com>  Fri, 30 Oct 2026 22:45:00 +0100

sssd-smartcard (0.3.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Reading a card now says which link in the chain broke, instead of blaming
    the card. 'cert inspect --from-card' and 'krb5 test --from-card' both
    failed with "no readable certificate found on the card" while 'card
    detect', run on the same host a minute later, reported one certificate
    found -- two commands in one tool disagreeing about one card. The card was
    held by a login or screen-lock helper at that moment, but the message said
    *no certificate*, which sends the operator to look at provisioning for what
    was contention.
  * The scan already knew: CardScan.checks carries the FAIL explaining which
    step failed, and the message discarded it. Three outcomes are now kept
    apart because they have three different remedies -- the card could not be
    read (retry, or find what is holding it; said explicitly when a card is
    present in the reader), the card was read and holds nothing (not
    provisioned), and the card holds certificates but none looks like an
    authentication certificate (choose one with --cert-id).
  * docs/sssd-reference.md records that Kerberos confirms the
    alternative-UPN-suffix distinction independently: kinit treats whatever
    follows the @ as a realm, so 'kinit someone@<upn-suffix>' fails with
    "Cannot find KDC for realm", while the real domain resolves. The suffix is
    a label on the account and nothing resolves it -- demonstrated by a tool
    that knows nothing about this project's certmap rule. To authenticate by
    the UPN instead, Kerberos has the enterprise principal mode, 'kinit -E',
    which is what 'krb5 test --enterprise' passes.

 -- Szymon Frankiewicz <szymonfrankiewicz@gmail.com>  Fri, 30 Oct 2026 22:15:00 +0100

sssd-smartcard (0.3.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Corrected a claim this project had made in three documents: that
    'sudo -l -U <user>' lists without authenticating. It does authenticate --
    'sudo -l' is a sudo command like any other and verifies the *invoking*
    user. What -U changes is whose rules are reported, not whether you prove
    who you are. The error came from measuring it as a service account with
    NOPASSWD, where the auth stack is skipped entirely, and generalising from
    that single observation. At a local console with a card it asks for the
    PIN, which is also the first direct sighting of sudo authenticating with
    the card locally rather than over a forwarded agent.
  * The paragraph immediately below it in DEPLOY.md -- that a password prompt
    is not evidence of having sudo rights, since sudo authenticates before it
    authorises -- was right all along and now reads consistently with the
    sentence above it.
  * The Kerberos section says which of its two findings were observed and
    which was derived. Both are now observed: klist in an SSH session, and
    separately in a local Wayland session on seat0, with loginctl confirming it
    was the local one rather than a remote shell misread as a console. The two
    paths have different causes, so one observation would not have covered
    both.
  * STATUS.md refreshed: every surface is now directly observed, and the open
    list distinguishes what was closed since the last snapshot -- the
    AutoloadCerts=1 and AgentFwd=0 branches, the HID Crescendo path with a
    second person's certificate -- from what remains asserted rather than
    measured: stock PuTTY's negative detection, MaxAuthTries exhaustion,
    touch-policy ALWAYS forcing a gesture, and the positive case of GSSAPI
    delegation.

 -- Szymon Frankiewicz <szymonfrankiewicz@gmail.com>  Fri, 30 Oct 2026 21:00:00 +0100

sssd-smartcard (0.3.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Both Windows guides state what SSH with the card does not give you: a
    Kerberos identity. The key is derived from the certificate, but public-key
    authentication never contacts the KDC, so the session holds no ticket --
    confirmed with klist inside a session. A reader who has just been told the
    card covers sudo as well is exactly the reader who will assume it covers
    Kerberos too, so the note sits beside the agent-forwarding material and the
    script prints a one-line pointer there under -ForwardAgent.
  * The section says plainly what would be required instead, and the caveat
    that decides whether that route is open at all: GSSAPI credential
    delegation forwards a ticket the client must already hold. Measured on the
    workstation in question -- not domain-joined, signed in with a local
    account, zero cached tickets -- so there was nothing to forward. With a
    domain-joined workstation and a card logon the authentication is then
    Kerberos rather than the card, the card having done its part one step
    earlier; that is a different path rather than an extension of this one.
  * It also repeats the measured finding that a console card login does not
    yield a ticket either, because local_auth_policy = enable:smartcard is
    local authentication and krb5-pkinit was not installed on the host -- said
    as the measurement it is, not as a general rule about PKINIT.
  * The guides point at docs/sssd-reference.md for the table across all four
    login paths rather than restating it, since duplicating a table is how two
    documents start disagreeing.

 -- Szymon Frankiewicz <szymonfrankiewicz@gmail.com>  Fri, 30 Oct 2026 20:00:00 +0100

sssd-smartcard (0.3.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * doctor says which login paths end with a Kerberos ticket, and which do not.
    "I logged in with the card" and "I have a Kerberos identity" are different
    statements, and a working Smart Card setup says nothing about the gap.
    Measured on a host where card login, SSH and sudo all worked: klist inside
    the session reported "No credentials cache found", and the host held no
    credential cache at all.
  * Two separate causes, neither a fault, so both are INFO. local_auth_policy =
    enable:smartcard is *local* authentication: SSSD verifies the certificate
    against the CA bundle and the certmap rule without contacting the KDC, so
    there is nothing to put in a cache -- a ticket needs PKINIT, which needs
    the MIT plugin, and on that host krb5-pkinit was not installed. Separately,
    SSH public-key authentication skips the PAM auth stack altogether, so
    pam_sss never runs and nothing obtains credentials either.
  * The hint names the password route as the alternative, because a password
    login does obtain a ticket through SSSD's Kerberos auth provider -- which
    is almost certainly what someone has already seen when they report that
    tickets sometimes appear. It also names klist as the way to confirm, and
    GSSAPI with credential delegation as the route if an SSH session genuinely
    needs one, with the caveat that delegation forwards a ticket the client
    must already hold.
  * Nothing here is reported as a failure. These are consequences of the chosen
    mechanisms, and the only reason to state them is that the consequence
    surprises people after everything else works.

 -- Szymon Frankiewicz <szymonfrankiewicz@gmail.com>  Fri, 30 Oct 2026 19:15:00 +0100

sssd-smartcard (0.3.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The Windows helper reports the PIV slot's PIN and touch policy, and
    explains what they mean for sudo. Asked by an operator coming from FIDO:
    "shouldn't sudo force a PIN prompt? With FIDO it asks every time."  It
    should not, and nothing in the output said why. Measured on the card:
    PIN policy ONCE, touch policy NEVER -- so the card is unlocked once per
    session and the signature pam_ssh_agent_auth asks for during sudo needs no
    new PIN. That is provisioning, not a weakness in the configuration. FIDO2
    differs by protocol: it requires user presence for every assertion.
  * The slot is found by matching the SHA-256 of the chosen certificate against
    what 'ykman piv info' reports per slot, never assumed to be 9A. When
    nothing matches, the policy is reported as unknown rather than another
    slot's being substituted -- verified by feeding it a store certificate that
    is not on the card.
  * Only a YubiKey can be read this way. For any other token the policy is
    unknown and the output says the vendor's own tooling is needed; no command
    is invented for one. The explanation prints on every path, including when
    the policy cannot be read, because the explanation is the part that answers
    the question.
  * INFO, never WARN: ONCE is the sane default and the overwhelmingly common
    provisioning, so it produces no action-list step. Flagging it would train
    people to ignore the line.
  * Both guides gained the same material under the sudo section, with the
    policy values (NEVER|ONCE|ALWAYS and NEVER|ALWAYS|CACHED, CACHED being a
    15-second window) and the constraint that both are fixed when the key is
    generated or imported -- so requiring a gesture per elevation means a new
    key and a re-issued certificate, not a configuration change.
  * Two check names stopped asserting the state they were testing. A failing
    run read "[FAIL] Saved session has agent forwarding enabled", which states
    the thing that is false and leaves the reader working out which half wins.
    The status carries the verdict; the name now carries only the subject.
    Found by exercising the AgentFwd=0 and AutoloadCerts=1 branches, which had
    never run: both were verified against the real registry values and the
    state restored afterwards.

 -- Szymon Frankiewicz <szymonfrankiewicz@gmail.com>  Fri, 30 Oct 2026 18:30:00 +0100

sssd-smartcard (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * sssd-smartcard-capture: take a working host's configuration and reproduce
    it on a new one. --from user@host collects over SSH (one login reused for
    every step, sudo there, nothing installed on the source); --local collects
    here; --show summarises; --replay drives sssd-smartcard-setup with what
    was captured -- a plan by default, --yes applies, --pam lets it change PAM.
    Never collected: keytabs, the SSSD cache, private keys, SSH host keys,
    /etc/shadow; sssd.conf options named *password*/*authtok*/*secret* keep
    their name and lose their value. Every file is checksummed at collection
    and a changed capture is refused. The domain join is deliberately not
    copied: the new host joins under its own name. What the replay does not
    reproduce is listed with the diff command for each file, and sssd.conf is
    compared key by key without host-specific and debug options.
  * Debian 13: libpam-sss 2.10 ships the sss-smart-card-optional/-required
    pam-auth-update profiles (measured in a debian:13 container), so the PAM
    plan is pam-auth-update there instead of a line to add by hand. Debian 12
    keeps the manual line. The setup script also trusts the profile files on
    disk when present.
  * sssd-smartcard-setup covers the rest of DEPLOY.md: stage revocation (one
    current CRL per CA and the refresh timer, before certmap so a profile that
    names crl_file never goes live without the file), stage ssh
    (AuthorizedKeysCommand sss_ssh_authorizedkeys, sshd -t before reload,
    sshd -T confirms no other file wins, sssd-ssh.socket), stage sudo (an AD
    group that must resolve, written through visudo; with --sudo-agent the
    env_keep line and pam_ssh_agent_auth with authorized_keys_command, the PAM
    part only with --pam), and --mkhomedir. Each takes its switch from the
    environment file too: crl, ssh, sudo_group, sudo_agent, mkhomedir.
  * With a profile given, the card stage no longer stops when no card is
    present: the profile needs no certificate to derive it.
  * docs/STEP-BY-STEP.md and docs/STEP-BY-STEP.pl.md: a plain, numbered guide
    in English and Polish -- copy a working host or start from scratch, where
    every file goes (the package, the capture, CA files, and on a Horizon
    image the agent archives), testing, and undo.
  * install.sh installs everything the .deb does: ad-prepare, ad-reset,
    sssd-smartcard-install-desktop, sssd-smartcard-capture and the shared
    helpers in /usr/share/sssd-smartcard/lib. Before, only the CLI and the
    setup script were installed, and the others could not find env.sh.

