sssd-smartcard (0.2.10) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The Windows helper gained an ordered action list, -Launch, and Polish.
    The remedies used to be printed beside each failing check, so a run that
    found three things left you scrolling back to reassemble them. They are now
    collected into one block built from the checks themselves -- a PASS
    contributes nothing, so the list carries exactly what this run found wrong,
    sorted by dependency rather than by check order. Steps are individually
    runnable: a multi-command step says whether its lines are one unit or
    separate commands, and the Startup shortcut is one line rather than four
    sharing a variable.
  * -Launch applies that list and connects. It refuses to install anything:
    winget downloads, installs and usually elevates, which is a different
    category from configuring what is already present, so a missing dependency
    stops it before connect with the install command printed and nothing
    changed. Every apply reads the value back through a fresh handle before it
    counts as done, and records the command to reverse it.
  * -Language en|pl, from a catalogue of 206 messages keyed by id. A missing
    translation falls back to English visibly; an unknown id returns a loud
    marker rather than a blank. Seven detection diagnostics are deliberately
    English-only: they are mostly binary symbols and exact option spellings,
    and they are what gets pasted into a bug report, where translating the few
    words around identifiers that must stay verbatim makes comparison harder.
    The file is now UTF-8 with a BOM, because PowerShell 5.1 assumes the ANSI
    code page without one and renders the diacritics as mojibake.
  * docs/windows-smartcard-ssh.pl.md and its PDF: the same material in Polish,
    with the dialogs described in words rather than shown, each description
    saying what the operator should see if things are right. Where a control
    label was not legible in the source material it says so instead of naming
    it.
  * A separate release artifact for the Windows side,
    sssd-smartcard-windows-<version>.zip, holding the script, the guide and a
    README. The person who needs those two files runs Windows and has no use
    for a Debian package; making them download the whole project to get a
    PowerShell script is how a script ends up being emailed around instead.
    The build refuses to write an archive that does not contain exactly those
    files, because a zip that looks like a release and delivers nothing is
    worse than no zip.
  * Three bugs the first HID Crescendo test found, all assumptions carried over
    from the only vendor previously available: "reader and card present" passed
    with every reader empty because it counted readers; the key container was
    parsed as a GUID, which a named container such as the Crescendo's does not
    match, so the comparison that exists to catch a stale store entry refused a
    perfectly good card; and the store-side read matched a regex against a whole
    multi-line blob, where PowerShell leaves $ anchored to the end of the string.
  * Two more found while verifying that work. The catalogue invariant test
    could be bypassed by writing an entry on one line: the parser skipped a
    shape it did not recognise and every test still passed. It now refuses when
    it finds an entry it did not itself read -- the guard on the guard. And
    -Reader naming an absent reader made the token section fall back to all
    readers while the certificate section filtered to zero and reported "no
    certificate could be read from any card", blaming the card for one it had
    read perfectly well and suggesting a minidriver install. Both sections now
    work from the same reader list.

