sssd-smartcard (0.2.9) unstable; urgency=medium

  * 'sssd-smartcard debug on|off|status|bundle'. Every failure in this project
    was found by raising debug_level, reproducing, reading the log and putting
    it back; doing that by hand on a host that is already half-configured is
    how a working sssd.conf gets broken. So the raising is a change like any
    other here: backed up, validated with sssctl config-check before the
    service is restarted, rolled back if it objects. The sshd half is a drop-in
    under sshd_config.d, validated with 'sshd -t' before the reload, so turning
    it down is deleting a file rather than editing the distribution's config.
  * doctor warns for as long as logging is raised. At level 9 SSSD writes the
    full base64 of every certificate it reads, on every authentication, which
    fills a disk on a host with real users -- and the operator who needed the
    logging is the one who will not remember it. Hexadecimal levels are
    understood, since sssd.conf accepts them and a decimal-only reader would
    report a raised host as quiet.
  * 'debug bundle' collects configuration, PAM stacks, SSSD logs, the journal
    for sssd/ssh/sudo, package versions, sssctl config-check, sshd -T and the
    effective sudo defaults into one archive, written 0600 -- created that way
    with os.open rather than opened and chmod-ed afterwards, which leaves the
    file world-readable for as long as it takes to write. What it never
    contains is listed in its own MANIFEST.txt: the keytab, /var/lib/sss/db
    (the cache holds credential material when cache_credentials is set),
    private keys, and the values of options named like secrets. Matching is on
    the option name rather than the value, so a password mentioned in a comment
    survives and a secret in an unfamiliar field is still caught. Certificates
    are included: they are public, and a bundle without them cannot answer the
    question it was made for.
  * The Windows helper and its guide cover preloading the certificate into
    Pageant, including the measured trap: -autoload does not load the saved
    list, it enumerates the entire certificate store and rewrites the list on
    every start -- 18 entries on the test machine, several with dead key
    containers. Since the agent offers keys one at a time and OpenSSH's default
    MaxAuthTries is 6, the right key may never be reached, and the symptom is
    "Server refused our key", which is also what three unrelated causes
    produce. AutoloadCerts=0 plus the thumbprint as a positional argument,
    through a Startup shortcut, loads exactly one.

 -- Szymon Frankiewicz <szymonfrankiewicz@gmail.com>  Wed, 21 Oct 2026 00:30:00 +0200

sssd-smartcard (0.2.8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * sudo can authenticate against a card held by an SSH client, and doctor now
    checks it. pam_sss cannot do this -- over SSH the token is on the client and
    the server never sees it, which SSSD states as "try_cert_auth flag set but
    no certificate available" before falling through to a password.
    pam_ssh_agent_auth closes the gap by verifying a signature from the
    forwarded agent. Measured end to end: "Running AuthorizedKeysCommand" ->
    "matching key found ... line 3" -> "ssh_rsa_verify: signature correct" ->
    "Authenticated", with no prompt at all.
  * The check distinguishes the sound configuration from the usual one. Most
    guides say to paste the public key into ~/.ssh/authorized_keys; that copy
    lives on the host and outlives revoking the certificate, so sudo would keep
    working after login and SSH had stopped. authorized_keys_command= pointed at
    sss_ssh_authorizedkeys asks the same source sshd asks, so one revocation
    covers all three. A static file is a WARN that says why; somebody else's
    key service is reported without a verdict.
  * Also checked: SSH_AUTH_SOCK surviving sudo's env_reset. Without it the
    module finds no agent and the stack falls through to the password prompt
    with nothing shown to the user -- a FAIL, because nothing else reveals it.
    Read from 'sudo -l', not 'sudo -V': the latter dumps settings only for root
    and 'sudo -n -V' is not valid usage at all, it prints the usage text, which
    a parser reads happily as "nothing preserved".
  * The Windows helper gained -ForwardAgent and -SessionName: it checks that
    Pageant is serving its named pipe (not the Windows OpenSSH agent pipe,
    which this build does not implement and which would report success with
    Pageant stopped), and that the saved session has AgentFwd and TryAgent set,
    with the reg add to fix them. It also reports the global AuthX509 value,
    which turns the "x509 is a global setting" claim into a checkable one.
  * Two misleading messages are documented in DEPLOY.md and the guide, because
    between them they cost an evening: "ssh-agent socket has incorrect
    permissions for owner" is what the module says for any reason it cannot use
    the socket, including a malformed SSH_AUTH_SOCK -- the mode sshd gives the
    socket is fine and should not be changed; and "Using default
    file=/etc/security/authorized_keys" appears even when authorized_keys_command
    is in use, because it reports the file field's default regardless of source.
  * DEPLOY.md step 8 covers who may run sudo, with the measured detail that a
    group name needs no quoting -- %"group@domain" is rejected as 'empty group'
    while the bare name and %#<gid> both parse -- and that a password prompt is
    not evidence of having sudo rights, since sudo authenticates before it
    authorises.

 -- Szymon Frankiewicz <szymonfrankiewicz@gmail.com>  Tue, 20 Oct 2026 23:45:00 +0200

sssd-smartcard (0.2.7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A Windows-side helper, scripts/windows/New-SmartCardSshCommand.ps1, which
    checks the workstation and prints the PuTTY command rather than running it.
    It verifies PuTTY-CAC rather than stock PuTTY (stock has no CAPI support at
    all), finds OpenSC, lists readers and cards, and -- the useful part --
    compares the key container named by the certificate in the Windows store
    against the containers actually on the card. A mismatch is what produces
    "the smart card cannot perform the requested operation or the operation
    requires a different smart card", and on the test machine 12 of 20 store
    entries reported Missing stored keyset. Yubico and HID Crescendo tokens are
    recognised by name; any other PIV token still works through a generic path
    rather than being refused, and only the minidriver suggestion is
    vendor-specific.
  * docs/windows-smartcard-ssh.pdf, with packaging/build-windows-guide.py to
    regenerate it from the Markdown source.
  * tests/test_env_init.py pinned the encoding across its subprocess boundary.
    Without it Python decoded the child's output with
    locale.getpreferredencoding(), so on a pl-PL Windows the non-ASCII
    assertion passed under Git Bash and failed under PowerShell: the test was
    measuring the console code page, not the loader. The scripts run on Linux,
    where the question does not arise, which is why the failure looked like a
    product defect and was not one. 541 tests now pass under both shells; the
    0.2.6 notes claimed 541 on the strength of one of them.

