sssd-smartcard (0.2.6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Check that sudo would actually consult SSSD. Two halves have to agree and
    neither complains when the other is missing: the 'sudoers: ... sss' line in
    nsswitch.conf, and the libsss-sudo package that implements that source.
    With the line alone sudo quietly falls back to the local file and every
    rule in the directory is ignored; with the package alone sudo never asks
    SSSD. Measured on the lab host, which had the package absent. A host using
    only local sudoers is reported as such rather than nagged, since that is a
    legitimate choice.
  * Check that sshd will accept a signature for the key types SSSD hands it. A
    client offering the right RSA key as x509v3-ssh-rsa was refused with
    'unsupported public key algorithm', and the client reported only "Server
    refused our key" -- the same words it prints when the key is not authorised
    at all. The server half is checkable: if none of a key type's signature
    algorithm names appear in PubkeyAcceptedAlgorithms, no client can ever
    authenticate with it. An RSA key passes when rsa-sha2-256 or rsa-sha2-512
    is accepted, since OpenSSH 8.8 dropped plain ssh-rsa from the defaults
    without dropping the key.
  * The GPO access control check moved into a function shared by doctor and the
    apply plan. DEPLOY.md claimed both warned about it; only the plan did.
  * DEPLOY.md documents connecting from Windows, which cost an evening:
    System32 ssh.exe accepts -I and silently ignores it (PKCS#11 compiled out,
    and -vvv shows it never even tries to load the library); PuTTY-CAC offers
    an RFC 6187 x509v3 certificate until -x509off is set, globally; and the
    certificate thumbprint must be read from the card, not from the Windows
    store, where an entry whose key container no longer exists produces
    "the smart card cannot perform the requested operation or the operation
    requires a different smart card" -- which is literally true.

