sssd-smartcard (0.2.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Warn when access_provider = ad has no ad_gpo_access_control. Measured: a
    card login whose auth phase succeeded was refused in the *account* phase,
    because ad_gpo_access_control defaults to enforcing and one unreadable
    Group Policy Container stopped the GPO list from being built ("Unable to
    get GPO list ... [14](Bad address)"). The greeter reported it as "smart
    card authentication didn't work", which sends the operator to the card.
    realm join writes access_provider = ad, so this is the default state of a
    freshly joined host and nothing in a Smart Card profile touches it. The
    profile model had carried a docstring warning about this default since it
    was written; it never reached an operator, because gpo.manage defaults to
    false and nothing said anything. The check reads the configuration as it
    will be after applying, so a profile that sets the option is not warned at.
  * Check SSH, which is a separate mechanism and not a consequence of the
    console login working. The token is on the client, so the server never sees
    a PIN and pam_cert_auth and local_auth_policy take no part; SSSD instead
    derives an SSH public key from the certificate in the directory entry and
    sshd asks for it through AuthorizedKeysCommand, which was unset on a host
    where card login worked. What does carry over is the part this tool
    configures: sss_ssh_authorizedkeys validates with ca_db and
    certificate_verification and filters through the certmap rules, so the same
    bundle, CRL and rule apply and revocation covers SSH too. doctor now
    reports the directive and how many keys come back for the user.
  * 'config apply', 'config diff' and 'config validate' print the renderer's
    notes. They printed its warnings and dropped its notes, so everything the
    renderer knows about an option it just wrote -- that SSSD never refreshes
    crl_file and needs the timer, that enabling OCSP does not relax the per-CA
    CRL requirement -- was visible in 'config render' and 'cert autoconfig' and
    invisible in the three commands that inspect or change a running system.
    tests/test_output_surfaces.py checks the shape rather than one message: a
    command that reaches for a rendered config's warnings and never for its
    notes has dropped them. That is what found config diff and config validate,
    which were not the command being fixed.
  * Both findings are documented with the measured evidence in
    docs/sssd-reference.md, and as steps 6 and 7 in DEPLOY.md -- including that
    'pamtester <service> <user> authenticate' tests only the auth phase and
    passed on the host where every real login was refused. The documented test
    is now 'authenticate acct_mgmt'.

